          "The most significant analytical gap in Mo-HD-11's data is the demographic one: the four reported Census categories (White 82.61%, Hispanic 6.09%, Black 5.15%, Asian 1.25%) sum to 94.1%, leaving a 5.9% unaccounted population. In Missouri's context, this gap most likely represents American Indian/Alaska Native residents, but it could also reflect mixed-race identification, immigration status variations, or Census methodology limitations. Critically, an unopposed race provides no electoral pressure to identify and engage this constituency—but any future challenger will have incentive to do so. More immediately, the district's economic profile—median age 41.4, median income $60,354, 33.5% renter rate—suggests a constituency vulnerable to economic messaging focused on household security: healthcare costs, childcare affordability, wage stagnation, and rental market pressure are the material concerns of a district where one-third of households do not own their homes and college education is out of reach for three-quarters of adults. The current 100% margin is an artifact of uncontested election, not a measure of durable Republican strength in a working-class district."
