          { name: "Wage Stagnation and Economic Opportunity for Low-Credentialed Workers", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 13.1% college attainment rate in a district with a 34.0 median age signals a workforce dominated by young adults without four-year degrees competing in a labor market increasingly requiring post-secondary credentials. At $32,671 median income, wage floors, apprenticeship pathways, community college affordability, and employer-training partnerships are the specific investments that determine whether young workers can build economic stability. The gap between credential requirements and community education capacity is the primary barrier to economic mobility for this district's demographic majority." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Affordability for Low-Income Communities of Color", tag: "careful", why: "A district that is 78.42% communities of color at $32,671 median income faces both the affordability barriers of a low-income constituency and the systemic healthcare disparities that disproportionately affect Black and Hispanic communities. Medicaid expansion, mental health and substance abuse treatment access, maternal health outcomes, and preventive care investment are the specific healthcare policy domains where this district's constituent needs diverge from statewide or national policy defaults. An incumbent who fails to center healthcare equity as a governing priority risks being outflanked by a challenger who treats healthcare disparities as a core constituent concern rather than an afterthought." },
          { name: "Treating 86% as a Durable Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "An 86% result in a low-income, renter-heavy district reflects the absence of credible opposition and inherited partisan loyalty, not evidence of constituent satisfaction with material conditions. This supermajority is durable only as long as opposition remains disorganized or ineffective; any challenger who articulates concrete, specific answers to the housing, wage, credential, and healthcare constraints this data reveals has the potential to shift the outcome significantly. Confidence in the 86% as a floor is the path to losing this seat to a challenger who actually listens to what constituents' economic lives require." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Intersection of Class and Racial Inequality", tag: "avoid", why: "A district that is 54% Black and 24% Hispanic at $32,671 median income with 13.1% college attainment lives with compounded barriers: the economic constraints of working poverty overlaid with the systemic discrimination in housing, employment, education, and healthcare that disproportionately shapes Black and Hispanic communities' outcomes. Any candidate who frames economic policy as class-neutral while ignoring the specific racial barriers that this district's majority faces will find that framing understood by constituents as evasion. Explicit engagement with racial equity in housing, employment, healthcare, and education is not a niche issue—it is the primary governing requirement in a district this demographically defined." }
