          { name: "Education Investment & Labor Market Alignment", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 64.3% college-educated adults — among the highest attainment rates in the state — Mo-HD-24 is a district where education is both a cultural value and an economic investment that constituents expect to yield career-track employment. State funding for higher education, workforce development alignment between universities and regional employers, and policy coherence between K-12, higher ed, and labor market demands are direct material concerns for a constituency that has invested in credentials and expects labor market returns. Candidates who can articulate how state policy ensures educated young adults find economic opportunity will build authentic connection to this constituency's core concern." },
          { name: "Demographic Visibility for 11% Gap Population", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for only 89% of the district's population — an 11% gap that likely represents additional Asian residents or mixed-race households, both rapidly growing constituencies in urban areas. In a heavily Democratic supermajority district, the risk is that smaller demographic groups become invisible within the larger coalition and their specific economic, cultural, and social concerns do not receive targeted constituent attention. Active engagement with Asian American communities, mixed-race constituents, and immigrant populations is both a governance obligation and an electoral reinforcement in a district where the 85% margin depends on consolidating diverse constituencies rather than a single dominant demographic." },
          { name: "Treating 85% as a Safe Majority That Does Not Require Active Service", tag: "avoid", why: "An 85-15 result is strong, but it reflects active Democratic alignment among urban, educated, young renters in this specific cycle — not a durable structural advantage that survives inattention. A legislator who assumes the 85% is a safe floor and reduces constituent engagement to performing partisan identity rather than delivering concrete policy outcomes for young renters, housing security, and economic mobility will find that the margin erodes. In heavily Democratic urban districts, the risk is not losing to the Republican opposition; it is losing to primary challengers or erosion to lower turnout when constituents conclude the incumbent is not delivering." },
          { name: "Positioning Urban Renter Majority Concerns as Special Interests Rather Than Core Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 72.7% renters with a 32.4-year median age, housing policy, tenant protections, and urban transit are not special interests — they are core constituent concerns for the district's demographic majority. Any candidate or legislator whose rhetoric or voting record positions renter advocacy, housing policy, or transit investment as serving a narrow constituency rather than the material interests of most people who live in the district will find that framing actively disconnected from the lived economic reality of Mo-HD-24's voters and will undermine otherwise strong Democratic performance." }
