          { name: "Working-Class Wage & Job Quality Standards", tag: "lean-into", why: "The combination of 21.9% college attainment and $65,663 median household income signals a working-class constituency largely dependent on service-sector, retail, and manual labor jobs that do not require four-year degrees—and that often provide limited wage growth or benefits. Advocacy for higher minimum wage standards, enforcement of labor standards in service-sector work, and incentive structures that reward quality jobs over job quantity are specific policy tools that directly address the economic trajectory uncertainty visible in the income data. Working-class voters evaluate whether government is on their side by whether it visibly fights for better wages and working conditions; symbolic representation without wage-standards advocacy reads as empty." },
          { name: "Black Community Investment & Accountability", tag: "careful", why: "At 37.77% Black population, the Black community is the numerical and political foundation of the Democratic coalition in HD-28, and the 68% Democratic margin reflects deep alignment between Black voters and Democratic representation. This relationship, however, remains conditional on whether the representative demonstrates visible, material investment in addressing the specific economic, healthcare, and social conditions affecting Black households—not generic diversity messaging or symbolic gestures. In a district where Democratic loyalty is the baseline, credible accountability mechanisms that allow Black constituents to evaluate whether their representative is delivering on specific commitments become the primary determinant of turnout and margin stability." },
          { name: "Overconfidence in the 68% Margin as Structural Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68% result in a district with working-class economic pressure, a 34.9% renter rate, and limited college attainment is not a structural supermajority—it is a working coalition that remains dependent on voters perceiving that Democratic representation translates into concrete improvement in their economic conditions. In lower-salience elections or against a challenger who focuses on economic frustration rather than cultural issues, this margin can compress 2-3 points toward the low 60s or even into the mid-50s if turnout drops among discouraged working-class voters. Treating 68% as a license to ignore constituent economic pressure or focus exclusively on identity-based messaging is the primary threat to the seat's durability." },
          { name: "Neglecting Younger Adult Economic Mobility Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "At 42.0 median age in a working-class district with $65,663 income and 21.9% college attainment, younger adults (25-40) are evaluating whether they can afford to stay in the district, whether they have access to credential pathways that lead to economic security, and whether local government is addressing housing and wage constraints or accepting them as inevitable. A representative who focuses on abstract Democratic positioning rather than tangible work on youth employment, education pathways, and housing affordability risks losing younger voter engagement and turnout, which directly affects the 68% margin in cycles where youth participation fluctuates. Younger working-class voters are less bound by inherited partisan loyalty and more likely to punish perceived economic neglect through disengagement." }
