          { name: "African American Base Consolidation & Demographic Coalition Building", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 27.41% Black population is the single largest demographic group and the electoral anchor of the 62% Democratic result; any erosion in African American turnout, enthusiasm, or candidate preference directly translates into margin loss in a 24-point diverse district. Simultaneously, the 51.28% white population includes working-class Democratic voters whose support is contingent on economic responsiveness but also includes Republican-leaning or persuadable swing voters who will evaluate on practical governance rather than party label. The incumbent's strategy must simultaneously deepen engagement with the Black community—showing measurable progress on criminal justice, economic opportunity, and community investment—while maintaining credible outreach to white working-class voters on shared economic concerns, avoiding any appearance of choosing racial justice messaging over economic messaging to the broader constituency." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Mobilization & Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 12.71% Hispanic population is substantial enough—roughly one in eight residents—that changes in Hispanic voter turnout or preference can materially shift a 62-38 margin; in midterm or lower-engagement cycles, Hispanic voter participation often declines, and if that decline is accompanied by swing toward a Republican challenger, the margin compresses quickly. The economic concerns of the Hispanic community—wage floors, employment discrimination, workplace conditions, healthcare access—are materially similar to those of Black and white working-class voters, but Hispanic voters in this district are not automatically consolidated and cannot be assumed to show up without targeted, culturally-grounded engagement that takes their specific economic concerns seriously." },
          { name: "Positioning Renter Advocacy as Radical or Class-Based Resentment", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 42.5% of voters rent and where housing affordability is a material household budget issue, a challenger's successful framing of renter-protective legislation or housing cost relief as 'anti-property rights' or 'class warfare' can be politically lethal to the incumbent's coalition. The incumbent must actively own tenant advocacy and housing affordability without positioning it as adversarial to property owners; the frame that matters is 'making housing work for working people,' not 'protecting renters from landlords.' If the incumbent allows a challenger to monopolize the framing of housing policy as a zero-sum conflict, they will lose the persuadable white voters who own property but also understand housing is unaffordable." },
          { name: "Treating a 62% Margin as a Stable Floor Rather Than a Contingent Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "A 62-38 result in a diverse, working-class district with below-average income, high renter concentration, and no dominant demographic majority is not a safe seat—it is a coalition held together by the incumbent's perceived responsiveness to material household economic concerns. Any incumbent who campaigns as though 62% represents a comfortable margin, or who allows their messaging to drift away from concrete economic results and toward cultural or ideological positioning, will discover that the margin is far more fragile than the headline suggests. In midterm cycles when presidential engagement declines, a 62% result in a renter-majority district can contract rapidly if the incumbent has not actively maintained the coalition's belief that their household situation is improving." }
