          "The strategic vulnerability of Mo-HD-40 is not immediate electoral competition — the seat is held reliably. The vulnerability is governance legitimacy: a representative who serves a district where roughly 5% of the population is unaccounted for in the Census data is not reaching the entire community, and in rural and semi-rural Missouri districts, that gap frequently represents Native American citizens whose relationship to state government, healthcare access, and economic development matters. The combination of low college attainment (15.2%), working-class income ($72,245 median), and high homeownership creates a constituency deeply concerned with property costs, local service delivery, and economic stability — issues that do not resolve through partisan messaging alone. An incumbent in an 83-17 district has the political space to invest in constituent service, engagement with undercounted communities, and addressing the material concerns of a working-class homeowner base without sacrificing the partisan margin. The most durable supermajority is one that is actively earned through governance that reaches the entire district, not one that is passively held by demographic advantage."
