          { name: "Consolidation of the Anglo Homeowner Base on Property Rights and Tax Limitation", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 87.9% white, 78% homeowners, 40.4 median age, and $81,688 income, Mo-HD-44 is precisely the demographic that votes Republican on property protection and tax limitation. The 65% result reflects alignment between the constituency's material interests and conservative messaging on these issues. Any candidate who can translate property rights, tax fairness, and residential neighborhood protection into concrete legislative action — school funding based on local accountability, property tax assessment fairness, zoning protection — reinforces the electoral coalition that makes 65% durable. This is not generic ideology; it is direct alignment between governance and constituent material interest." },
          { name: "Young Adult and Renter Economic Concerns", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 22.2% renter rate and 35% Democratic opposition suggest a constituency of younger renters and working-age households for whom housing affordability, wage growth, and healthcare access are more salient than property-tax limitation. At a $81,688 median income district-wide, renters are likely clustered at the lower income distribution and facing housing-cost burden in proportion to their incomes. A candidate who can address renter concerns with specificity — housing supply policy, zoning reform that reduces housing costs, or wage-competitiveness initiatives that improve entry-level economic mobility — is building beyond the existing 65% rather than writing off 35% of the district as inherently opposition." },
          { name: "Understanding the 35% Opposition in a High-Income, College-Educated District", tag: "careful", why: "In a district this prosperous and educated, 35% opposition is not a fringe or ideological opposition — it represents college-educated professionals, working-age households, and families with specific governance grievances about healthcare delivery, education quality, environmental protection, or social policy. The opposition is not economically marginal; it is embedded in the same community. A representative who never engages the opposition's actual concerns and instead assumes they are culturally lost is missing the opportunity to understand what 35% of their educated, economically substantial constituency actually needs from state government. This opposition is capable of organizing and mobilizing, particularly if a challenger addresses their concerns." },
          { name: "Assuming the 65% Margin is a Safe Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 65-35 result in a state as red as Missouri can appear comfortable, but it reflects a contested coalition held by alignment on specific issues rather than demographic inevitability. The combination of 22.2% renters, 42.3% college-educated adults, and a 35% Democratic opposition suggests that demographic change, renter turnout, or a credible challenger who addresses renter and young-adult concerns could shift the margin downward. A candidate who campaigns as though 65% is a safe majority and consolidates only the existing base while neglecting the 35% is not building durable electoral strength — they are managing an inherently fragile coalition." },
          { name: "Treating the ~7.95% Demographic Gap as Politically Invisible", tag: "avoid", why: "The gap between reported Census categories and 100% represents Hispanic, multiracial, and other non-Anglo residents who exist in the district but are not highlighted by the headline demographics. In a high-income district, these residents are not economically marginal — they are part of the professional, working, and small-business community. A representative who treats this ~8% as politically irrelevant or culturally separate rather than as constituents integrated into the economic community is forgoing relationship-building with part of the district and creating space for a challenger to build authentic engagement with these communities. Even small shifts in support or turnout among this constituency could materially affect a 65-35 margin." }
