          "The district's real strategic vulnerability lies in two constituencies that are present but often invisible in a purely homeowner-focused legislative agenda. First, the roughly 11.3% minority population (4.45% Hispanic, 4.19% Black, 2.64% Asian) has material economic concerns — business opportunity, educational equity, professional advancement, and housing affordability — that do not automatically align with affluent Anglo homeowner priorities. Second, the 15.7% renter population and younger adults in the 38.7 median age range face different economic pressures: student debt, entry-level job quality, and housing affordability are their primary concerns, not property tax management. A representative who builds active constituent relationships with these communities — not token acknowledgment but genuine engagement on their economic priorities — both improves constituent service and builds durable political protection against future opposition. The 100% result creates space to govern for the entire district, not just the demographic majority."
