          "The absence of a challenger in 2024 reflects the district's Democratic alignment and the incumbent's current political standing, but it provides no guarantee of durability if governance fails to address the economic security concerns of a 62% Black, 49% renter, $46K-income constituency. Working-class renters in low-income districts evaluate their representatives on school funding, police accountability, healthcare access, and whether utilities, housing, and basic services are becoming more or less affordable. In a district this economically pressured, these are not abstract policy areas — they are the lived experience of daily household management. An incumbent who uses unopposed status to build active relationships with neighborhood organizations, workforce-development providers, landlord-accountability advocates, and educational institutions is investing in the constituency relationships that insulate against future challenge. An incumbent who treats unopposed status as permission to maintain distance from constituent organizing or to treat economic-security issues as peripheral rather than central is making a strategic error. The demographic supermajority is large, but it is also organized around material economic concerns that require active, visible legislative response."
