          { name: "Police Accountability & Public Safety", tag: "careful", why: "Urban working-class communities in the Kansas City metropolitan area have diverse positions on police reform — ranging from public safety investment to accountability — that do not track simplistically along partisan lines. At 34.7 median age with young families in a $54,910 income bracket, both public safety and police accountability are genuine community concerns requiring nuanced engagement rather than ideological positioning. An incumbent who dismisses either concern as secondary risks alienating significant portions of the Democratic coalition." },
          { name: "Positioning as Opposition to Republicans in an 88% District", tag: "avoid", why: "In MO-HD-78, opposition messaging against Republicans is not strategically relevant because the Republican Party is not a competitive actor in this district. An incumbent who relies on anti-Republican rhetoric rather than demonstrating delivery on housing, education, economic opportunity, and public safety for Black and working-class constituents is avoiding accountability for actual governance performance. This rhetorical strategy in a supermajority district signals that concrete policy wins are absent — and opens space for primary challengers." },
          { name: "Assuming Supermajority Status as a Durable Base", tag: "avoid", why: "An 88% margin in a primary-driven Democratic district is not a durable coalition — it is a verdict on the incumbent's delivery to Black and working-class renter communities. Incumbent complacency that assumes the 88% reflects stable support rather than active constituent satisfaction creates vulnerability to primary challengers who position themselves as more responsive to housing, education, safety, and economic mobility concerns. In a supermajority district, the only strategic error worse than assuming you are untouchable is actually becoming untouchable to your own voters' material needs." }
