          { name: "Wage-Level Employment and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 59.5% of adults lacking college credentials and median income at $47,178, MO HD-79's working-class majority depends on wage-level employment opportunities and pathways to economic mobility that do not require four-year degrees. Job training infrastructure, apprenticeship programs, small business support for entrepreneurs in the district, and accountability for whether local employers are offering wages that allow households to afford housing and healthcare are the concrete economic policy concerns that matter most to this constituency. An incumbent who demonstrates specific knowledge of the district's employment landscape and can articulate concrete job-creation strategies is more credible than one who relies on abstract economic messaging." },
          { name: "Unopposed Status as Strategic Vulnerability Rather Than Strength", tag: "careful", why: "Unopposed elections frequently mask incumbent complacency and deterioration of constituent engagement infrastructure — when a credible challenger emerges, the incumbent discovers that years of running without opposition have atrophied turnout mobilization, constituent service responsiveness, and active voter relationship-building. In a district where 65.3% of households are renters at $47,178 income, voter activation depends on demonstrated delivery on economic concerns, not partisan loyalty alone. An incumbent who treats the unopposed 2024 result as evidence of sustainable security rather than absence of organized opposition is strategically vulnerable to a well-resourced challenger in a future cycle." },
          { name: "Treating Partisan Dominance as Substitute for Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "The 100% Democratic result in 2024 reflects the district's partisan composition, not necessarily the incumbent's constituent service record or whether the representative is addressing renter households' immediate economic concerns. In a constituency where 65.3% are renters and median income is $47,178, voter participation is contingent on perceived responsiveness to housing affordability, wage-level jobs, and healthcare access — not inherited Democratic registration. An incumbent who relies on partisan advantage rather than demonstrating concrete improvement in renter households' economic conditions is vulnerable to a challenger who campaigns on material economic concerns, regardless of district Democratic composition." },
          { name: "Assuming College-Educated Constituency Reflects District Priorities", tag: "avoid", why: "The 40.5% college attainment rate means 59.5% of adults lack college credentials — a working-class majority whose economic concerns are not about higher education access alone but about wage-level employment, affordable housing, and healthcare that does not depend on professional-class income. A representative who frames policy priorities around college affordability and degree pathways while neglecting renter household economic security, wage-floor employment, and affordable housing is misallocating attention away from the district's actual majority constituency and their most immediate concerns." }
