          { name: "Treating a 77% Margin as Indicating Complete Coalition Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "A 77% margin is structurally durable but can mask specific pockets of unmet need or demographic segments that receive less constituent attention than the dominant college-educated professional base. Renters (41.3%), service-sector workers, communities of color (though white at 78.65%, the district still contains meaningful Asian (4.81%), Black (6.67%), and Hispanic (2.2%) populations), and younger adults with specific economic concerns can become invisible when the overall margin is this large. Allowing these constituencies to feel neglected or taken for granted is the primary way a 77% seat becomes vulnerable — not through loss of the core coalition but through reduced turnout or a credible challenger's message on specific neglected issues." },
          { name: "Over-Indexing on Progressive Issue Positioning at the Expense of Fiscal Responsibility Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "MO-HD-83's college-educated, affluent constituency votes Democratic on social and environmental issues but evaluates on economic competence, fiscal responsibility, and whether government spending delivers measurable results. A legislative record or messaging platform that emphasizes progressive positioning on social issues while appearing indifferent to cost-effectiveness, budget discipline, or whether state programs are delivering results creates an opening for a Republican challenger to argue that Reed is spending affluent households' tax dollars on ineffective programs. In a district with this income level, voters have the option to support Republicans if they conclude Democrats are fiscal underperformers, even if they disagree with Republican social policy — avoiding this vulnerability requires balancing progressive messaging with clear evidence of fiscal competence and program effectiveness." }
