          "Engage the 41.3% renter constituency with specific policy attention to housing affordability, student debt, and entry-level wage growth; renters in affluent districts often have lower turnout than homeowners and can become swing voters if they perceive that Democratic governance is not delivering on material economic concerns that directly affect their quality of life.",
          "Monitor the 23% Republican opposition for signs of consolidation or growth; in a district this heavily Democratic, a shift from 23% to 28-30% would still be a safe hold, but it would signal either weakness in a specific demographic segment (affluent business owners, suburban professionals, younger renters) or a shift in national issue salience that requires a localized response.",
          "Build constituent engagement infrastructure that reaches beyond the college-educated professional base to ensure that service delivery, responsive constituent services, and visibility extend to renters, service-sector workers, and households without college credentials who live in the district but may not be part of the dominant demographic narrative — neglecting these constituencies can erode margins in future cycles when national dynamics are less favorable."
