          "The primary strategic imperative is economic credibility. At $54K median household income with 60% of households renting, affordability and wage growth are not peripheral issues—they are the baseline of voter concern. A candidate must articulate concrete, specific steps on housing (rent stabilization, development incentives, tenant protections) and job quality (wage floors, apprenticeships, credential pathways for non-college workers). The second imperative is equity and trust, especially on public safety and policing. With 57.11% Black constituents, framing matters enormously: generic tough-on-crime messaging will alienate; community-centered accountability and prevention investment will resonate. The third is turnout protection. An 88% result is strong but not invulnerable if younger voters or renters perceive that the incumbent is not delivering on their material needs. Campaign efforts should prioritize direct contact with renters and workers under 40, emphasizing concrete wins on housing and jobs."
