          { name: "Fiscal Discipline & Property Tax Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $127,549 median household income with 80.6% homeownership, MO HD-90 is a constituency of property-holders for whom annual tax bills are material household budget items despite high income. Local government and school district fiscal management—the capacity to deliver services without creeping tax increases—is a direct economic concern, not an abstract principle. A candidate who demonstrates specific competence in managing public budgets, controlling cost growth, and avoiding tax-rate creep will find resonance with this constituency, particularly if the opponent is vulnerable on fiscal discipline or has a record of budget mismanagement." },
          { name: "Stability of Affluent Suburban Democratic Alignment", tag: "careful", why: "The 61% Democratic result in a district this affluent and educated reflects conditional rather than durable partisan alignment; college-educated suburban professionals have demonstrated the capacity to shift between parties when they perceive governance failure or when economic conditions create tax pressure. The 39% Republican base in a district this prosperous is not a peripheral minority—it represents a fully engaged, high-propensity voting population with organized infrastructure and resources. Any incumbent who treats the 61% as a stable floor rather than a dependent variable of education and infrastructure performance will find the margin compressed in a cycle where opposition is credible on fiscal discipline." },
          { name: "Adopting Ideological Positioning in Place of Local Governance Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "MO HD-90's constituency evaluates on concrete delivery—school outcomes, infrastructure quality, neighborhood safety, responsive local service—not partisan ideology or cultural positioning. A candidate who defaults to abstract ideological argument rather than specific answers to education funding, property tax burden, and infrastructure investment will lose credibility with voters who have the resources to demand and expect competent, responsive governance. In an affluent suburban district, ideological purity is less persuasive than demonstrated competence." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 39% Opposition as Peripheral or Unchangeable", tag: "avoid", why: "The 39% Republican result in a district this affluent and educated represents 39% of a highly engaged, organized, high-propensity voter population—not a marginal or disengaged opposition. These are homeowners, business professionals, and educated voters with the resources and motivation to fund and organize alternative candidacies. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns as though this opposition is peripheral or safely contained misreads the district's actual political composition and vulnerability. A well-positioned Republican who accepts the district's cultural liberalism while emphasizing fiscal discipline will find real traction among affluent suburban professionals who remain ideologically conservative." }
