          { name: "Affluent Homeowner Economic Security and Tax Burden", tag: "lean-into", why: "Mo-HD-91's $100,474 median household income is the highest benchmarked, with 71% homeownership and 62% college attainment — a classic affluent suburban constituency for whom property tax burden, mortgage costs, school funding quality, and infrastructure investment are primary material concerns. Doll must deliver visibly on these issues and position herself as the candidate who understands that affluent homeowners' support depends on demonstrable value return, not partisan default. Any Republican challenger will make tax burden and fiscal responsibility central to their case; preempting that argument with a specific legislative record on property tax fairness and efficient government spending is essential to holding this seat when it becomes contested." },
          { name: "Education Quality and School Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 62% college-educated constituency with 39.7 median age suggests a significant proportion of parents with school-age children for whom K-12 educational quality is a primary driver of neighborhood choice and property values. School funding equity, teacher compensation, and curriculum quality are not abstract policy issues in Mo-HD-91 — they are the direct determinants of whether parents stay in the district and whether property values hold. Doll's legislative focus on education funding and her visibility in school communities is both constituent service and electoral durability, as education quality is one of the few issues on which affluent suburban voters will abandon partisan loyalty if they perceive the other party is better positioned to deliver." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity and Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 7.9% demographic gap, combined with Black (7.4%) and Hispanic (5.15%) populations in a district where white residents comprise 78%, means meaningful minority communities whose concerns can become invisible in a majority-white affluent area. While these communities may not be numerically dominant, their economic mobility, educational access, and healthcare concerns are real constituent needs that deserve specific legislative attention. Doll's engagement with these communities — not as a voting block to be taken for granted, but as constituents with specific governance needs — is both a test of genuine representation and electoral insurance against future demographic shifts that might make a broader coalition necessary." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Evidence of Permanent Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in 2024 tells us that Republicans did not field a challenger in that cycle — not that Republicans cannot or will not field a credible challenger in 2026 or beyond. Affluent suburban districts have become the primary terrain of Republican growth in the past decade, particularly on tax and government efficiency messaging. Any incumbent who interprets 100% as a floor rather than a moment to consolidate will be vulnerable when a well-resourced challenger emerges with a message on fiscal responsibility and property tax management." },
          { name: "Assuming Affluent Voters Are Ideologically Locked Democratic", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent, highly educated homeowners are not partisan ideologues — they are pragmatists who support candidates they believe will deliver on property values, school quality, and tax fairness. In the past decade, Republican gains in affluent suburban areas have come precisely from candidates who separated fiscal conservatism from cultural polarization and made property value protection and efficient government their central case. Doll's brand and message must emphasize delivery on material homeowner concerns, not partisan identity or cultural positioning, to remain credible to a constituency that will defect if it believes the other party will better serve its economic interests." }
