          { name: "Education Funding & Suburban School System Performance", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 38.5% college education and $75,000 median income in a 25.6%-renter district, educational outcomes and school system quality are directly tied to property values and family economic investment. The 54% Democratic margin in an otherwise Republican-trending affluent suburban district suggests education-focused messaging resonated; a candidate cannot hold or win this seat without credible specificity on K-12 funding, teacher compensation, and measurable academic outcomes. Any retreat on education investment or perception of declining school quality is the most direct threat to the Democratic incumbent's 8-point margin." },
          { name: "Local Service Delivery & Government Efficiency", tag: "lean-into", why: "Affluent suburban voters at 42.6-year median age and 74.4% homeownership evaluate government primarily on competence and service delivery—road maintenance, public safety response times, permit processing, zoning fairness—not partisan ideology. The 54% result in a district trending Republican suggests the incumbent has successfully positioned as a competent local manager; losing that perception is losing the seat. A challenger must articulate specific service improvements or governance failures; an incumbent must defend tangible service-delivery credibility with concrete metrics." },
          { name: "Non-College Working-Class Economic Anxiety", tag: "careful", why: "The 61.5% non-college majority in HD-92 is the constituency most vulnerable to economic populism and anti-establishment messaging, yet the 54% Democratic result suggests Burton secured meaningful support here. This constituency—homeowners facing property tax pressure, inflation-driven service costs, and wage stagnation relative to cost of living—is not ideologically locked into either party but evaluates on whether government is delivering economic stability for them. Losing these voters to a Republican framing of tax burden or government waste is the most direct path to flipping the seat; holding them requires specific economic messaging on cost-of-living issues, not abstract appeals to Democratic values." },
          { name: "Deploying Racial Justice Framing in a 87.6% White District", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 12% combined minority population deserves specific policy attention and genuine constituent service, a Democratic candidate who leads with racial justice messaging in a district that is 87.6% white and $75,000 affluent—rather than on education, service delivery, and economic competence—is actively alienating the non-college white working-class constituency that holds the swing votes. This is not a minority-majority district; treating it as one rhetorically while 87.6% of voters are white creates a credibility gap that directly undermines the education and service-delivery framing that actually won 54% in 2024." },
          { name: "Assuming Suburban Education-Based Democratic Coalition is Durable Without Active Defense", tag: "avoid", why: "Education-college-educated suburban voters are in genuine flux nationally; the cohort that voted Democratic on education grounds in 2020-2024 is not a permanent Democratic constituency if economic messaging, local school controversies, or national partisan shifts reframe education policy. A 54% incumbent who does not actively maintain persuasive contact with both the college-educated base and the persuadable non-college majority will find the margin collapses in a cycle where Republican messaging successfully frames education, school choice, or tax burden differently. The 8-point margin is a floor only if actively defended." }
