          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security and Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $61,929 median income with 24% renters in a district where homeownership is 76%, housing affordability and wage sustainability are the material foundation of constituent satisfaction. The 23.6% college-educated rate signals a working- and middle-class constituency whose economic mobility depends on wages keeping pace with housing costs and healthcare expenses — not abstract economic metrics. Legislative focus on housing cost drivers, workforce development that connects to actual jobs, and healthcare access in the district's specific labor market is what translates a 69% margin into durability rather than treating it as permission to govern without reference to constituent economic conditions." },
          { name: "Understanding the 31% Opposition and Demographic Shift Risks", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 94.99% of the district population, leaving approximately a 5% gap — likely Native American residents whose political behavior and engagement levels are not fully captured in the partisan data. In a district with emerging Hispanic (4.22%) and Black (4.15%) communities alongside the 82.93% white majority, demographic change is incremental but real; Moore's 69% may reflect strong performance among white working-class voters that should not be assumed as permanent if economic conditions deteriorate or if minority community outreach is deprioritized. The opposition is small enough not to present immediate electoral threat but large enough to become a problem if it organizes around specific grievances — the representative should understand what drives the 31% and actively address those concerns rather than ignoring them." },
          { name: "Governing as Though 69% Is a Mandate Without Checking Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "Supermajorities create the psychological trap of believing the margin reflects approval of performance rather than alignment of identity or opposition weakness — a 31% opposition in a Democratic district may reflect both genuine dissatisfaction and the absence of a credible Republican alternative, not endorsement of the incumbent's legislative record. If Moore governs the next two years without systematically measuring whether working-class constituents feel their economic security is improving, housing affordability is getting better, or wages are tracking with cost of living, the margin can erode rapidly when a competent challenger emerges or when economic conditions shift." },
          { name: "Treating the District's Demographic Complexity as a Given Rather Than a Strategic Asset", tag: "avoid", why: "A district that is 82.93% white but includes emerging Hispanic and Black communities with distinct economic and healthcare concerns, plus an approximately 5% gap likely representing Native American residents, requires proactive multicommunity constituent engagement — not default governance that assumes a white working-class base will remain stable. If Moore's legislative record and constituent contact strategy do not actively demonstrate service to the Hispanic, Black, and Native American constituents in the district, those communities remain mobilizable by future opposition or may simply withdraw participation. The 69% is not a reason to reduce community engagement; it is a reason to deepen it and make it specific to each constituency's actual concerns." }
