          { name: "Understanding the 38% Democratic Opposition Coalition", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district at $107,210 income and 47% college attainment, the 38% Democratic vote cannot be explained by lower income or lower education — the opposition is organizing on issues and values, not demographics. Understanding specifically whether that coalition concentrates among younger professionals, college-educated women, voters prioritizing healthcare access, or those frustrated with K-12 education governance is essential to competing for the seat. The 24-point margin suggests the opposition is smaller and possibly more concentrated than the Republican base, which means targeted engagement with specific opposition sub-constituencies may be more efficient than a broad base-consolidation strategy." },
          { name: "The 5% Demographic Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 94.96% of HD-95's population, leaving approximately 5% unaccounted for — substantially smaller than in tribal-heavy districts but nonetheless present. In a district this overwhelmingly white (90.09%), that gap is unlikely to represent a large alternative constituency, but identifying who that 5% is and whether they represent a swing population worth mobilizing is part of understanding the district's actual composition. Any candidate who assumes HD-95 is purely the four reported categories may miss a small but real constituency with specific needs or concerns." },
          { name: "Treating 62% as a Safe, Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where the opposition is college-educated and high-income, and where the opposition coalition is concentrated rather than distributed across all demographics, assuming that 62% represents a stable floor is strategically dangerous. College-educated suburban voters shift their allegiances based on issue performance and governance delivery, not inherited partisan loyalty — any incumbent or challenger who consolidates only the existing Republican base without addressing why educated professionals are voting Democratic will find the margin compressed when opposition candidates effectively message on education, healthcare, or local governance." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Aging Population's Healthcare and Retirement Security", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-95's median age of 48 and high income ($107,210) create a constituency with specific and present concerns about healthcare costs, Medicare sustainability, long-term care planning, and retirement security — issues that often crosscut partisan identity among older affluent voters. Any candidate who treats healthcare as an abstract policy debate rather than as a direct material concern for a 48-year-old, high-income population is missing the constituency's most pressing practical governance priorities. Healthcare cost and access failures by current officeholders are among the most potent swing factors in affluent aging districts." }
