          { name: "Defaulting to Generic Anti-Government Messaging in a Highly Educated District", tag: "avoid", why: "College-educated suburban voters at $113,776 income are sophisticated consumers of political messaging who can distinguish between fiscal conservatism and anti-government ideology, and they increasingly reject the latter. Generic messaging about government overreach and regulatory burden without specific problem-solving on issues that affect their lives — housing policy, transportation, environmental management — reads as ideologically driven rather than leadership-oriented. In a district where voters have the education and resources to evaluate governance carefully, messaging that respects that sophistication will outperform messaging that assumes voters will accept ideology as substitute for policy." }
