          { name: "Social or Cultural Polarization as a Primary Campaign Frame", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74% Republican margin in a rural, homeowner-dominant, low-education district does not require cultural warfare to hold — it is grounded in material economic concerns (property taxes, healthcare costs, government waste) that a candidate can address directly. Leaning into divisive social or cultural issues as a primary campaign frame risks alienating the persuadable 26% and activating opposition mobilization among younger adults or minority communities who perceive the candidate as more interested in conflict than constituent service. The 74% is stable when the incumbent demonstrates competence on cost-of-living issues; it becomes vulnerable when the candidate substitutes cultural grievance for practical governance." }
