          { name: "Treating 72% as a Durable Electoral Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 72-28 result in a district with a 25.2-year median age, 49.6% renter rate, and $68,624 household income is not a durable supermajority built on stable interests — it is a coalition built on current incumbent performance and working-class consolidation that can shift rapidly if economic conditions worsen or a credible challenger emerges with specific answers to young renters' material concerns. Any incumbent who treats 72% as permission to govern without active constituent engagement on economic issues is likely to see that margin narrow substantially in a competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Educational Diversity of the District", tag: "avoid", why: "At 32.2% college-educated, MO-HD-121 has a substantial population without college degrees — approximately 67.8% — but also a meaningful educated minority that may have different economic concerns and policy priorities than the working-class majority. Any candidate who frames the district as uniformly working-class or who defaults to education-skeptical positioning risks alienating the college-educated segment and missing opportunities to address the specific workplace and economic concerns of younger adults with degrees facing underemployment or credential-inflation challenges. The district's education mix requires nuanced messaging, not one-dimensional framing." }
