          { name: "Working-Family Economic Security and Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $55,039 median income with 41.9% renters and 34.3 median age, Mo-HD-122 is a working-family constituency for whom cost-of-living management is a direct household concern. Healthcare affordability, property tax or rent burden, utility costs, and wage growth relative to inflation are the material economic drivers that shape voter behavior in this income band — not cultural abstractions. A representative who addresses these concerns with specific policy solutions (healthcare access improvements, utility affordability programs, wage-supporting economic development) builds credibility with the constituency that generates the 73% margin; one who defaults to generic conservative rhetoric misses the core economic anxiety that drives the 27% opposition and vulnerabilities in a contested race." },
          { name: "Scrutinizing the 73% Result and Incumbent Advantage", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 73% Republican margin in a district with 33% college attainment, $55,039 income, and 41.9% rental rate is unusually high for Missouri's current political environment and warrants analytical investigation. The margin likely reflects Peters' incumbent advantage, name recognition, and constituent service effectiveness rather than a structural 73-27 district alignment — meaning the seat's true competitive baseline is unknown and potentially lower than the 2024 result suggests. Any challenger should test whether Peters' margin collapses with serious candidate recruitment and campaign investment, while any incumbent must recognize that the 73% may be an inflated floor that requires sustained constituent engagement to maintain in a genuinely contested cycle." },
          { name: "Minority Community Engagement and the 5.5% Demographic Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories account for 94.5% of the district's population; the remaining 5.5% is likely American Indian/Alaska Native residents or other communities whose political orientation and constituent needs remain unclear from the data. While no individual non-white community (Asian 3.68%, Hispanic/Latino 3.03%, Black 2.15%) is large enough to swing the seat, their collective representation and the unmeasured 5.5% gap mean that deliberate engagement or deliberate invisibility becomes a governing signal. In a district where the Democratic opposition is capped at 27%, these communities are unlikely to be the pathway to challenging the current Republican hold — but they are a measure of whether representation is genuinely inclusive or reflexively partisan." },
          { name: "Overconfidence in the 73% Supermajority as Structural", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73% result is not predictive of a 73-27 structural alignment in a district with this demographic and economic profile; it is much more likely to reflect incumbent advantage, challenger weakness, or local campaign dynamics than settled voter preference. Any candidate — incumbent or challenger — who treats the current margin as a stable floor rather than a volatile result dependent on campaign quality and incumbent performance will misjudge the district's true competitiveness and make strategic errors that create openings for a more serious opponent in the next cycle." },
          { name: "Treating Renter Households as a Peripheral Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "At 41.9%, renters represent a substantial portion of Mo-HD-122's population and are disproportionately younger, working-class households for whom housing costs, tenant protections, and economic mobility are direct concerns. Any candidate whose constituent service, policy messaging, or legislative record treats renters as peripheral to a 'homeowner-focused' political agenda will find that 41.9% increasingly responsive to challenger messaging about cost-of-living pressure and economic fairness — making this constituency an underutilized vulnerability in what currently appears to be a safe Republican seat." }
