          { name: "Cost of living and household economics", tag: "lean-into", why: "Median household income below $52,000 paired with a 26.2% renter rate signals financial pressure on a significant portion of households. Many residents are likely living paycheck-to-paycheck, making property taxes, housing costs, and utility expenses central to electoral behavior. Concrete messaging on tax relief, affordable housing preservation, and wage stability will resonate; avoid abstract economic theory." },
          { name: "Immigration and demographic change", tag: "careful", why: "While Hispanic/Latino representation is only 2.64%, the presence of demographic diversity and the rural character suggest this is a community historically homogeneous and potentially sensitive to rapid change. This is not a high-conflict area on ethnic grounds based on current numbers, but candidates must be measured: nativist rhetoric risks alienating younger, more diverse-minded voters without gaining net support, while pro-diversity positioning may feel disconnected from local experience." },
          { name: "Climate regulation and environmental restrictions on business", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with working-class household incomes, production-based economy, and low college attainment typically views environmental compliance as a cost burden rather than an investment. Strict emissions or land-use regulations risk being framed as urban-imposed constraints on local livelihoods. Given the incumbent's overwhelming 82% margin, defending existing business operations and resisting new regulatory burden is safer ground than championing environmental enforcement." },
          { name: "Urban-centric social issues (DEI initiatives, urban transit, progressive cultural priorities)", tag: "avoid", why: "The demographic profile—92.46% white, 41.3 median age, 19.1% college education, $51,337 median income—is inversely correlated with constituencies where progressive social messaging drives turnout. These voters prioritize economic survival and stability, not identity politics or cultural signaling. The 82% Republican margin reflects values alignment; messaging on urban-style social initiatives will be seen as wasteful or culturally foreign and should be avoided entirely." }
