          { name: "Cost of living and household economics", tag: "lean-into", why: "Median household income of $67,582 with 32.8% renters indicates significant financial pressure on working families and those without home equity. The 68-point margin suggests the incumbent has successfully framed economic messaging, but a challenger must address affordability directly—property taxes, housing costs, and wage stagnation—to crack into the 32% Democratic base and peel off persuadable swing voters who are economically stressed despite the district's Republican lean." },
          { name: "Rural infrastructure and service delivery", tag: "lean-into", why: "The Ozark Plateau region with median age 34.8 and moderate education levels (28.6% college-educated) typically depends on local government for water, roads, broadband, and emergency services. This is a foundation issue for both parties in non-urban districts; the 68% Republican margin means the incumbent owns this terrain, but any challenger who can demonstrate superior competence on pothole-to-broadband issues can credibly contest the seat by earning trust on tangible delivery." },
          { name: "Education funding and K-12 outcomes", tag: "careful", why: "With only 28.6% college-educated adults, this district has not prioritized higher education, but public K-12 schools are critical infrastructure and political flashpoint. The 68% margin suggests Republican messaging on education has won decisively, likely via school choice, parental rights, or fiscal restraint frames; a Democratic challenge that appears to advocate significant tax increases for schools or cultural curriculum changes will reinforce the incumbent's advantage, not erode it." },
          { name: "Gun control and Second Amendment restrictions", tag: "avoid", why: "The 87.04% white, rural character of the district and the overwhelming 68-32 partisan split indicate strong Second Amendment support and skepticism of gun regulations. Any candidate who emphasizes stricter gun laws or frames firearms as a public health crisis will activate Republican turnout and alienate the persuadable middle; this is a losing wedge in this specific district." },
          { name: "Immigration and border policy as primary campaign focus", tag: "avoid", why: "Hispanics comprise only 4.6% of the district, and the 68-point margin suggests the district is not competitive on cultural grievance messaging around immigration. Overemphasis on this issue by a challenger will appear tone-deaf to the district's real economic concerns and will reinforce the incumbent's advantage by suggesting the challenger is out of touch with local priorities; resources are better spent on issues with direct household impact." }
