          { name: "Wage & Worker Economic Mobility for Low-Income Constituencies", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 25.5% college attainment and $42,226 median income, HD-133's constituency is predominantly working-class adults and young adults in early-career positions facing limited wage growth and economic advancement constraints. Minimum wage, worker protections, job quality, and pathways to economic mobility are concrete governance concerns that directly affect household budgets. A candidate who addresses these material concerns with specificity—whether through legislative record or credible commitment—connects with the economic reality of the district's majority constituency more effectively than abstract fiscal positioning." },
          { name: "Minority Community Responsiveness & Economic Inclusion", tag: "careful", why: "At 6.58% Hispanic, 4.65% Black, and approximately 7% other (Asian, Native American), HD-133 has a meaningful minority constituency that, at this income level, faces compounded economic pressure around job access, healthcare, and housing. The 44% Democratic opposition likely includes substantial minority voter concentration; a candidate or incumbent who treats minority economic concerns as subsidiary to the majority white constituency's priorities will not consolidate or gain minority support. Authentic engagement on minority economic mobility, healthcare access, and employer discrimination is both a governance obligation and an electoral necessity in a district this economically constrained." },
          { name: "Treating a 56% Margin as a Durable Majority in an Economically Precarious District", tag: "avoid", why: "The 56-44 result in a district with $42,226 median income, 63.7% renters, and 31.5-year median age is not a stable majority—it is a plurality held by incumbent reputation and a 44% opposition that represents a genuine organized constituency. Any candidate who campaigns this seat as though 56% represents a safe hold, or who defaults to consolidating the existing Republican base without actively addressing the renter majority and low-income workers' material concerns, will find the margin at risk in a contested cycle. This is a seat that requires active constituent engagement on economic security, not confident assumption of loyalty." },
          { name: "Ideology-First Messaging in a Constituency Evaluated on Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "At $42,226 median income and 63.7% renter rate, HD-133 voters evaluate candidates primarily on concrete economic delivery—housing affordability, wages, healthcare access, cost of living—not ideological positioning. Any candidate whose messaging defaults to abstract conservative or progressive principle, rather than specific solutions to the material economic concerns of working-class renters, misses the actual decision-making criteria of this constituency. Voters at this income level and renter status are pragmatic; they support whichever candidate credibly addresses their economic security, regardless of party label or abstract ideology." }
