          { name: "Rural Service Delivery & Infrastructure Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "Working-class households at $58,090 income depend directly on local service quality—roads, water and sewer systems, rural broadband access, school funding, emergency services—because they lack the resources to privately substitute for inadequate public infrastructure. A representative who can point to specific wins on broadband expansion, road maintenance, school funding increases, or rural healthcare access demonstrates that government investment is flowing to the district and improving material quality of life. Unopposed victories often mask local frustration with service failures or perceived neglect; building a record of visible infrastructure wins is the primary way to inoculate against future opposition mobilization." },
          { name: "Hispanic and Native American Community Economic Participation", tag: "careful", why: "With 9.47% Hispanic and approximately 15% unaccounted population (likely including Native American residents and other minorities), Mo-HD-159 contains meaningful minority communities whose economic concerns—agricultural and service-sector wage stagnation, healthcare access, workforce opportunity—deserve specific constituent attention rather than absorption into a generic working-class narrative. These communities are capable of meaningful electoral mobilization if they perceive the incumbent or a challenger as addressing their specific economic and social concerns; a representative who engages them with specificity builds political durability rather than assuming they are automatically part of the Anglo working-class coalition." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Durable Support", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed win is often a sign of weak opposition recruitment or timing, not genuine voter enthusiasm or satisfaction—and in a district with lower education levels and real working-class economic anxiety, it can mask latent frustration that mobilizes quickly when a credible challenger emerges. The 100% result says nothing about whether working families believe their property taxes are fair, their healthcare costs are manageable, or their children have real economic opportunity. An incumbent who treats unopposed victory as evidence of durable support and reduces year-round constituent engagement is vulnerable to sudden competitive threat." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning Over Material Economic Solutions", tag: "avoid", why: "Working-class households at 17.1% college attainment and $58,090 income do not organize their political preferences around abstract conservative or progressive ideology; they organize them around whether their property taxes are affordable, whether they can access healthcare without bankruptcy, whether their children can find good jobs without leaving the district. A candidate or incumbent who relies on partisan tribal signaling or ideological rhetoric rather than specific policy solutions to local economic problems will find that positioning ineffective with a constituency that evaluates on material outcome. In a future competitive race, the candidate who leads with concrete solutions will command credibility; the one who leads with ideology will be vulnerable." }
