          { name: "Treating 52% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52% result in a district with 36.5% renters and $62,209 median income is not a safe majority — it is a plurality of contested voters in a working-class constituency that has demonstrated substantial willingness to vote against the incumbent party. The 48% opposition in a district with these economic characteristics is not a ceiling; it is evidence of a constituency evaluating on economic delivery rather than partisan loyalty. Any incumbent or challenger who campaigns this seat as though 52% represents a comfortable hold is arithmetically and strategically wrong." },
          { name: "Allowing Renter & Hispanic Community Concerns to Become Invisible", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 36.5% are renters and 15.05% are Hispanic, failing to specifically address the economic concerns of these communities is leaving roughly half the constituency unrepresented. Renters and Hispanic households have distinct but overlapping concerns — housing affordability, wage fairness, childcare access, healthcare — that require specific policy attention rather than absorption into generic working-class messaging. A candidate who defaults to abstract positioning on 'pro-business' or 'tax relief' without demonstrating concrete delivery to renters and Hispanic constituents will find these communities mobilizing around an alternative candidate." }
