          { name: "Healthcare Access and Affordability for Affluent Professionals", tag: "lean-into", why: "In a district where median income is $90,774 and college attainment is 41.6%, healthcare costs and access are real constraints even for affluent households — and where Democratic messaging on healthcare affordability has gained significant ground among professional voters. The incumbent should address healthcare policy with specificity toward this constituency: prescription drug costs, insurance premium trends, rural healthcare viability (if relevant), and Medicare/insurance regulation that affects working professionals and near-retirees. Ceding healthcare messaging to opposition framing leaves a core affluent constituency concern unaddressed." },
          { name: "Understanding the 45% Opposition in a Prosperous Republican District", tag: "careful", why: "A 45% opposition vote in a district with $90,774 median income and 82.14% white population is an unusual signal in a Republican-leaning state; it suggests concentrated concern among college-educated voters rather than socioeconomic or demographic disadvantage. The incumbent should identify specifically whether the opposition reflects healthcare cost anxiety, education quality concerns, fiscal management perception, or a shift in how professional voters evaluate candidates on issues like environmental stewardship or governance competence. Understanding the driving concerns behind the 45% is essential to preventing margin erosion in a subsequent cycle." },
          { name: "Assuming 55% Represents a Stable Electoral Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "At 55-45, Schnelting holds the seat, but the margin is not commanding — and college-educated suburban voters have demonstrated significant willingness to shift between parties when they perceive candidate or governance issues warrant it. If the incumbent treats 55% as a durable majority and reduces constituent engagement, or if a credible challenger emerges with stronger positioning on healthcare, education, or fiscal management, the margin is at genuine risk of significant erosion in a subsequent cycle." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Minority (23%) and Demographic Diversity Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "While MO-SD-23 is 82.14% white and 77% homeowner, the 23% renter population and smaller minority communities (5.79% Black, 4.59% Hispanic, 2.95% Asian) represent voters whose economic concerns and policy priorities may differ from the homeowner majority. The renter constituency, even if demographically white and college-educated, faces different material constraints (housing costs, lease stability) than homeowners, and minority communities have distinct concerns around economic mobility and social policy. Any incumbent who campaigns only to the homeowner Anglo majority misses the opportunity to consolidate or narrow opposition among these constituencies." }
