          { name: "Cultural and Social Values", tag: "careful", why: "The district is 90.31% white with very low racial diversity (4.05% Hispanic/Latino, 1.07% Black, 0.45% Asian, ~4% Native American). Messaging on cultural identity and social norms carries outsize weight in such homogeneous constituencies. However, unopposed incumbency and low educational attainment suggest voters are not highly polarized on identity issues—overemphasis risks seeming performative or divisive without winning additional support." },
          { name: "Gun Control and Second Amendment Restrictions", tag: "avoid", why: "Rural districts with high homeownership, low density, and modest incomes typically view firearms as tools for livelihood (hunting, predator control) and self-reliance. Without explicit data on urban/rural split, the Ozark region context and median age of 42.6 (stable, long-tenure residents) suggest deep cultural attachment to Second Amendment rights. Restrictive messaging alienates core constituency with no offsetting voter gains." },
          { name: "Tax Increases on Middle-Income Households", tag: "avoid", why: "A median income of $53,876 places most households in the working-to-middle-income band where tax burden is acutely felt. With 17.2% college education and limited higher-wage job density, households lack the income elasticity to absorb new levies. Unopposed victory suggests voters are not actively seeking redistributive tax policy; messaging on tax burden reduction or resistance aligns better with baseline sentiment." }
