          "The renter rate of 55.5% is the single most important number in this district's strategic profile. In a constituency with a $46,891 median income and 34.6 median age, this represents younger adults and families navigating rental markets without sufficient accumulated equity to own homes — and therefore experiencing housing costs that are likely consuming 30-40% or more of monthly income in a state with rising rental rates but stagnant wage growth. The 58% Republican result should not be misinterpreted as a secure base; it is a working-class coalition that has not yet decided it has a better alternative. If the incumbent Republican is visibly aligning with policy positions that increase housing costs, reduce renter protections, or ignore childcare and affordability pressures, the 42% opposition has a concrete entry point for expansion. Conversely, if the Republican can position themselves as defending working-class economics against cost-of-living pressures — whether through property tax policy, utility regulation, or housing supply advocacy — the margin becomes genuinely durable. The district is not ideologically locked; it is economically driven."
